From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The Playport authors <dev@playport.dev>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Arm64: complete a split atomic without its alignment fault on
 iOS

A locked x86 operation on a slot that crosses 16 bytes is an ARM64
atomic that crosses 16 bytes, which takes an alignment fault on an iOS
host. Its round trip, the Mach exception, the BSD signal, the Windows
exception and FEX's handler, then NtContinue, costs 35 to 55 us an
operation, and a CASAL was emulated apart from the others, on the Mach
exception thread, outside FEX's StrictSplitLockMutex.

On an iOS host the JIT now checks the address of each 16-, 32- and
64-bit CASAL and LSE atomic memory op before issuing it. One that
crosses 16 bytes calls a dispatcher stub instead: it saves x0-x30, sp,
q0-q31 and NZCV, and HandleSplitAtomicFromJIT completes the same
instruction with the same CAS helpers the fault path uses, under
StrictSplitLockMutex, writes its result register, and the stub returns
past it. Every split CASAL and atomic memory op the JIT issues now takes
that one lock. The inline check is three instructions that change no
flags. CASPAL (cmpxchg8b/16b) keeps the fault path.

Class: feature
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-10-13-split-lock-fast-path.md
Offered-upstream: no; an iOS host path, FEX's Windows frontend has no iOS host upstream
